      "writtenOn": "2026-07-14T10:40:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`postponed_per_vm_setup()` is called unconditionally for every vcpu and we return from `postponed_per_vm_setup()` after taking the lock and checking if `pkvm_vm-\u003epostponed_setup_done`. But the snapshoting of `irqchip_mode` and `max_vcpu_ids` happens before that. So a malicious host can have the first `__vcpu_create` succeed and call it again for subsequent cpus with invalid `irqchip_mode` or `max_vcpu_ids`. This will call `postponed_per_vm_setup()` unconditionally and fail before checking `pkvm_vm-\u003epostponed_setup_done`. That was the case I was talking about as a host can intentionally fail the vcpu creation even though we have snapshotted valid values for `irqchip_mode` and `max_vcpu_ids` during first vcpu creation. A malicious host can create a VM with failed vcpus and let the VM run with the initially created vcpu alone. With my limited understanding, this cannot cause any security issues, just a DoS. Was referring to this.",
